.TH VOICE 2
.SH NAME
voice \- a neural voice model, held open
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "voice.m";
voice := load Voice Voice->PATH;

MODEL: con "/lib/ml/voices/en_US-lessac-low.onnx";
RATE:  con 16000;

Speaker: adt {
	dir:  string;
	ctl:  ref Sys->FD;
	rate: int;

	speak:   fn(s: self ref Speaker, phonemes: string): (array of real, string);
	lookup:  fn(s: self ref Speaker, sym: string): int;
	feedids: fn(s: self ref Speaker, ids: array of int): (array of real, string);
};

init:     fn(): string;
open:     fn(model, units: string): (ref Speaker, string);
play:     fn(pcm: array of real, rate: int, out: string): string;
pcmbytes: fn(pcm: array of real): array of byte;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Voice
loads a neural
.SM VITS
model through
.IR ml (3)
and keeps it loaded, so that speaking a second thing does not mean paying to
load a sixty-megabyte model a second time.
.IR Say (1)
is built on this; anything that has more than one utterance to make in one
run should be too.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once before
.BR open .
It loads the libraries this module uses internally and returns nil, or why it
could not.
.PP
.B Open
loads
.I model
.RI ( nil
for
.BR MODEL )
onto
.I units
.RI ( nil
for
.BR "cpu" ,
see
.IR say (1)
for why that is the default) and returns a
.B Speaker
with the model's own symbol table already read, or nil and why not.
That is the expensive part, and the whole reason to keep the result: on this
machine it costs around half a second, against tens of milliseconds for an
utterance once it is open.
.PP
.B Speak
takes phonemes \(em the symbols
.IR phon (2)
produces, the same ones
.I say
takes with
.BR -p ,
and returns the samples, between -1 and 1, or nil and why not.
The arranging the model wants \(em begin, then every symbol followed by the
pad symbol, then end \(em happens here, because it is identical for every
utterance and is not worth asking every caller to get right by hand.
.PP
.B Lookup
turns one symbol into the model's number for it, or -1.
.B Feedids
is the model's wire with no arranging at all: whatever ids are given go
straight in, unwrapped.
.B Speak
builds its begin/pad/end sequence and calls this to run it; it is exposed
because
.IR say (1)'s
.I id...
form wants to show that sequence rather than have
.B speak
build it invisibly.
.PP
.B Play
writes samples to
.I out
.RI ( nil
for
.BR /dev/audio ),
as signed 16-bit little-endian.
Where
.I out
is
.B /dev/audio
it first writes
.I rate
and one channel to
.BR /dev/audioctl ,
because the device infers nothing from what is written to it; a file just
holds the samples, at whatever rate the caller remembers to name.
Closing what it opened is what waits for the sound to finish \(em a write only
queues it \(em so
.B play
returns when
.I out
is
.BR /dev/audio
and the utterance has been heard, which is what makes a caller's pacing come
out right when it speaks several things in a row.
.PP
.B Pcmbytes
is the same conversion
.B play
uses, exposed for a caller that wants the bytes without writing them anywhere
yet.
Samples are clamped to the 16-bit range rather than wrapped: a sample past 1.0
is
.IR loud ,
not a different sign.
.SH "WHY HOLD IT OPEN"
.IR Say (1)
used to load the model, speak one utterance, and exit, which costs between one
and two seconds whatever is said.
That is invisible in a narrated demo with pauses already built into it, and
hopeless for anything with several lines to say in a row: a script that says
eight things used to load the model eight times for no reason, since nothing
about the model changed between calls.
.PP
.IR Say (1)
now opens one
.B Speaker
and calls
.B speak
once per
.I -t
or
.I -p
given, in the order given, so a script with several lines pays the model's
cost once.
Measured: three utterances through one
.B say
invocation took 0.6 seconds; the same three utterances through three separate
.B say
invocations \(em the old behaviour \(em took 1.6, and that difference is
.I understating
the saving, because it also includes starting
.IR emu (1)
three times rather than once.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/voice.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR speakertest (1),
.IR phon (2),
.IR ml (3),
.IR audio (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every call returns a string saying what went wrong rather than raising.
.B Speak
and
.B feedids
name the phoneme with no symbol in the voice, or the underlying
.IR ml (3)
failure, rather than a generic refusal.
.SH BUGS
A
.SM VITS
voice is stochastic by design \(em the three
.I scales
.B speak
and
.B feedids
fix are literally named
.IR say (1)'s
"how noisy, how fast, how variable" \(em so calling
.B speak
twice with identical phonemes gives audibly similar but numerically different
samples each time.
A test that expected the same bytes back found this out the first time it was
tried, failing on the first sample by under a thousandth: that is the model
working as designed, not a bug in either
.B speak
or
.BR feedids .
.PP
One model per
.BR Speaker ,
open for as long as the caller holds it; there is no way to ask for a
different voice without a second
.BR open .
Two
.B Speaker
s can be open at once \(em each is its own
.IR ml (3)
instance \(em but that is two models' worth of memory, tens of megabytes
apiece.
There is no explicit close: dropping the last reference to a
.B Speaker
drops the last reference to
.BR ctl ,
and closing that is what frees the
.IR ml (3)
instance, so it happens whenever the collector gets to it rather than the
moment a caller is done \(em ordinary for anything in this tree that holds a
file open, and not particular to this module.
.PP
.B Play
to a file truncates it.
A caller that speaks several utterances to the same file, rather than to
.BR /dev/audio ,
gets only the last one \(em
.IR say (1)
does exactly this with
.B -o
and says so in its own BUGS.
